<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Zell am Harmersbach</h1><p class="st-meta">1366 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Zell am Harmersbach</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of the smallest free imperial cities, a Black Forest town in the Harmersbach valley that kept its independence until 1803.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zell-am-harmersbach">Zell</a> grew in the Harmersbach valley near the abbey of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-gengenbach">Gengenbach</a> and was recognised as a free imperial city in the 14th century, with its status confirmed in 1366. With the Free Imperial City of Gengenbach and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-offenburg">Offenburg</a> it lay within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ortenau">Ortenau</a> bailiwick, which emperors often pledged to neighbouring lords.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>With only a few hundred inhabitants, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zell-am-harmersbach">Zell</a> was among the tiniest imperial estates, governed by a council and mayor. The town and the neighbouring free valley of Harmersbach, an unusual imperial community of peasants, lived from forestry, farming and crafts. Zell remained Catholic.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The mediatisation of 1803 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zell-am-harmersbach">Zell</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>. Its pottery works, founded in 1794 and later known as Zeller Keramik, made the town's name in the 19th century. It is now a small town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-imperial-city-of-zell-am-harmersbach.jpg" alt="Free Imperial City of Zell am Harmersbach" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Holger Uwe Schmitt · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:15.7.2019_Besuch_in_Zell_am_Harmersbach._03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1366 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zell am Harmersbach</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Alemannic German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zell am Harmersbach</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1584&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-zell-am-harmersbach" aria-label="Free Imperial City of Zell am Harmersbach on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4867 487 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free Imperial City of Zell am Harmersbach lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5187 743L5229 694M5187 743L5194 723"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5194 723h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5187 743h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5187 743h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1584&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-zell-am-harmersbach">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
